Z||000003||rogergarrison||07-20-2001||08:02 PM||||first see if the hood-fender gap is even. If its closer towards the windshield than at front, loosen the 10MM bolts along the inside edge of fender where the hood opens. you can pull it back out slightly then retighten the bolts, should clear door then.<P>If that gap is even, all the adjustment bolts are at the door hinges. you can move the top of the door back or forward fairly easy and the same for the bottom. The other bolts will move the door in or out at the hinge. best to mark exactly where the hinges are before you loosen them so if you get it all messed up at least you can start back where you were.  If its hitting determine if its level with the fender, then the door needs moved towards rear of car. if its sticking out slightly past the fender it needs moved in towards the windshield post. If it needs moved in or out its lots harder than back or forward. the bolts to move it in or out can only be loosened after the interior door panel is removed, so it may be easier to move the fender out a little instead even if the gap at hood widens slighly. very very little movement make a big difference so do it in tiny steps at a time. only way to get it right is trial and error. after your done adjusting you may have to adjust the striker (the big thing like a bolt sticking out of the rear of the door jam) it has to line up with the latch groove in the door itself to close and latch properly.you just loosen it and move it around till it hits where its supposed too. good luck||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||07-21-2001||11:36 AM||||First thing to check is the door skin. There is some room for them to move. If the skin was removed from the door it may be slightly out of position on the door body.<BR>||||reg||1
